  "query_preview": "..\n\n- Meteorological Overview:\n\nBehind a cold front that pushed through the area overnight, it \nhas been notably cooler across North Central and Southwestern \nMontana today with most areas on the plains and in the valleys \nstruggling to get out of the 50s and 60s as of early afternoon. \nWhile an isolated shower (or even a rumble of thunder) cannot be \nentirely ruled out across the plains this afternoon, most of us \nshould remain a bit dry, albeit breezy through the remainder of \nthe day thanks to dry air moving into the area. \n\nWeak upper level ridging will begin to transverse the Northern \nRockies on Monday, bringing a day of seasonable temperatures and \ndry weather to the area as it moves overhead. However, the ridge \nshifts further east on Tuesday, which will allow some moisture to \nsneak into Southwestern Montana as early as Tuesday afternoon \nresulting in some showers and thunderstorms. Further north, \nTuesday will still be seasonably warm with hiss in the low/mid 80s\nand generally sunny skies.\n\nShowers and thunderstorms become a bit more widespread Wednesday \nand Thursday as our next upper level trough moves in from the west\ncoast, which will also bring some increased wind gusts to \nSouthwestern Montana as it does so. While these winds won\u2019t be \nparticularly noteworthy on their own, combined with some drier air\nwill result in some elevated fire danger across portions of \nGallatin and Madison Counties. \n\nNext weekend will be dictated by how exactly an upper level low \noff the west coast/Pacific Northwest behaves, and there seems to \nbe some uncertainty as to how exactly it will behave. See the \nConfidence and Scenarios section below for more details.\n\n - Forecast Confidence & Scenarios:\n\nWinds Wednesday and Thursday: as a series of upper level troughs \nembedded in persistent upper level southwesterly flow moves \noverhead, winds will increase, primarily across Southwestern \nMontana Wednesday and Thursday. While the wind increase looks \nlikely, the magnitude, extent, and duration of the winds are \nlikely to be quite sensitive to the exact track of these \ndisturbances. As of Sunday, the NBM probabilities seem to indicate\nthat there are roughly similar probabilities for wind gusts over \n45 mph, with chances around 50% generally confined to some of the \nNorth/South oriented valleys of Southwestern Montana near and \nsouth of Interstate 15. \n\nNext Weekend: There are a few ways next weekend could pan out, \nlargely driven by how strong an upper level trough gets over the \nwest coast and whether or not it \u201ccuts off\u201d from the flow and \nbecomes a closed upper level low. There does seem to be a little \nmore support for the latter scenario (around 75% of the ensemble \nmembers), but its strength and location will play a large role in \nthe details of our weather, and models seem to be in quite a bit \nof disagreement with these particulars. Should the low/trough wind\nup staying offshore, next weekend is likely to be a bit on the \nwarmer side of the forecast, with upper level ridging building in \noverhead. However, a more eastward placement of this low/trough, \nwhich is around 60% of the ensemble members would result in a \ncooler, more unsettled result for next weekend\u2019s weather. Ludwig",
